From 57050394f102990e0b61aec762219f1b5ab5a474 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: bluezr <bluezr@dogecoin.com>
Date: Fri, 28 Aug 2026 16:57:28 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 4/7] psbt: let a consumer read back what the setters put in

The struct is not in the installed header set and there were no accessors, so
of the 23 public PSBT entry points every one wrote or transformed and none
read. A consumer could set a redeem script, sign, combine and extract, and
could not learn how many inputs a PSBT it received even had.

That is survivable while the library finalizes for you. It is not once the
caller supplies its own scriptSig, which the previous commit allows: building
one means reading the partial signatures and the redeem script the signers
left behind, and both were unreachable. The finalizer hook shipped without
them would have been unusable.

Adds counts, version, partial signature count and accessor, redeem script for
inputs and outputs, final scriptSig, and sighash type. Buffer-and-length
throughout so no internal type reaches a signature and the struct stays
opaque. A NULL buffer reports the length needed and returns false, so callers
size then fetch.

test_psbt_accessors signs, reads the signature back through the accessor,
assembles a scriptSig from it, installs it with set_final_scriptsig and reads
that back byte for byte, without touching the struct. It fails on line 1548
with the partial signature copy stubbed. Covers the size-query path, short
buffers, out-of-range indices and NULL. 103/103.
